\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.16}{\ignorespaces Batch normalization compared with domain-specific batch normalization, from Chang et al.~\cite  {chang2019dsbn}. (a) A single BatchNorm layer processes source ($x_S$) and target ($x_T$) examples alike, so its statistics describe a mixture of the two domains. (b) A \acs {DSBN} layer carries one branch per domain, and each input is routed to the branch corresponding to its own domain; the other branch, shown greyed, is inactive for that example. All parameters outside the \acs {DSBN} layers remain shared across domains. In this thesis the source domain is Sentinel-2 and the target domain is $\Phi $-sat-2, and the domain of every input is known both during training and on board. }}{38}{figure.caption.34}\protected@file@percent }
\AC@undonewlabel{acro:DSBN}
